# There was no .gitignore here at all, and the two things that would have been
# committed first are the two that must never be.
#
#   .env        scripts/cf.sh sources it for CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, which is a
#               credential for the whole account.
#   .wrangler/  holds the local D1 database, which after any real use is a file
#               full of customers' names, addresses and coordinates.
#   .dev.vars   `wrangler dev` reads it and its values OVERRIDE the [vars] in
#               wrangler.toml, which is exactly why it is dangerous to commit.
#               wrangler.toml tells a developer to put their own
#               ALLOWED_ORIGINS there — "http://localhost:5173" — so that the
#               dev origin lives on the dev machine and never reaches a
#               deploy. Committed back, it is the same hole with an extra
#               step: a localhost origin echoed with
#               access-control-allow-credentials in production. It is also
#               where anyone's local API keys end up. Note that `.env.*` above
#               does NOT cover this name.
.env
.env.*
!web/.env.production
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
.wrangler/
node_modules/
web/dist/
*.log

# Build and tool output. `tsc -b` writes the buildinfo next to web/tsconfig.json
# and it is a cache keyed to one machine's absolute paths; seed/ is what
# scripts/build-postal-codes.mjs generates, and a single country's file runs to
# hundreds of megabytes of SQL.
web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
seed/
art-source/
